-- 0029: full alias-directory audit — retire verified false pairs.
-- Audit: workspace/threads/main/alias-audit-complete-20260813/
-- Applied 2026-08-13 with explicit Bhoumik approval; returned exactly UPDATE 4654.
-- Active pairs 13,553 -> 8,899. Scale is comparable to the 2026-07-31 cleanup,
-- not to the focal 0021/0025/0028 sets.
--
-- REGRESSION (before -> after), 20 searches:
--   unchanged, as required: acer palmatum 1781/35/336, rosa 1905 vs 1918 rows
--     but 76 suppliers both ways, live oak 339/45/41, palms 2448/68/582,
--     crape myrtle 616/38/163, loropetalum 269/27/118, drift rose 69/9/35,
--     knock out rose 42/8/23, bloodgood japanese maple 183/28/8,
--     white fir 32/5/8, soft leaf yucca 11/7/6 (0028 holds)
--   rescued pairs held: dusty miller 8/5/6, blue weber agave 13/4/4,
--     river birch multi trunk 11/5/1
--   wrong-taxon removals landed: norway maple 16 -> 13 rows / 2 -> 1 supplier
--     (Acer truncatum gone), monkey puzzle 9 -> 6 / 3 -> 2 identities
--     (Araucaria bidwillii gone)
--   cultivar-link removals landed: nikko blue hydrangea 20 -> 14,
--     hydrangea macrophylla 133 -> 129, prunus persica 155 -> 141
--   red yucca 57 -> 54: the three lost rows are generic Hesperaloe funifera
--     admitted through the retired `RED YUCCA LITTLE GIANT` cultivar-links.
--     Every genuine H. parviflora row still returns, and Verde River's row that
--     literally prints RED YUCCA LITTLE GIANT still matches on its own name.
--
-- Note that bare-hub searches barely moved (acer palmatum unchanged despite 75
-- retired edges; rosa -13 rows despite 187). Those rows match their own printed
-- names directly, so the aliases were redundant for FINDING them and load-bearing
-- only for the false cross-matches. That is the outcome to want.
--
-- Prompted by Chris's "soft leaf yucca" false positive (see 0028). That bug was
-- one bad edge; this is the directory-wide sweep for the same defect classes.
--
-- METHOD
--   Pass 1 — 20 reviewers covered all 13,553 active pairs. The windows were
--     proven to tile the corpus exactly once: every flag was mapped back to its
--     position in the canonical (name_key, other_name_key, id) ordering and
--     checked against its reviewer's bounds. The first run had a real defect —
--     concurrent agents corrupted each other's cached tool results, so three
--     windows collided and two windows (positions 678-1355 and 10170-10847)
--     went unreviewed while the row total still summed to 13,553. Those were
--     re-run against explicit boundary ids and re-verified. Result: 4,993 flags.
--   Pass 2 — 13 adversarial verifiers re-examined every flag, prompted to
--     REFUTE and told to default to "keep" when torn, because a wrongly retired
--     alias breaks a real search silently while a wrongly kept one stays
--     visible. 264 flags (5.3%) were refuted and are retained.
--   Held back — 75 further pairs carrying low confidence or an `unsure`
--     category are NOT retired here; see held_back.csv.
--
-- RETIRING 4654 pairs, leaving 8,899 of 13,553 active (-34.3%):
--   cultivar-link    3,981   a cultivar/selection/series/trade name against a
--                            bare species, so every generic row of that species
--                            answers a search for the one selection
--   data-not-name      275   patent brands, grade codes, truncated fragments
--   generic-category   194   catch-all labels fitting many distinct plants
--   wrong-taxon        204   simply different products by the buyer's test —
--                            e.g. Abies alba <-> White Fir (that is A. concolor),
--                            Acer truncatum <-> Norway Maple (A. platanoides),
--                            Araucaria bidwillii <-> Monkey Puzzle (A. araucana)
--
-- Most contaminated hubs: Rosa (187 edges), Acer palmatum (75),
-- Hydrangea macrophylla (45), Loropetalum chinense (44), Prunus persica (43).
--
-- REGRESSION REQUIRED before commit — run regression-queries.sql in the audit
-- directory and compare identities, not just counts. Retiring a species<->
-- cultivar edge deliberately stops a bare-species search reaching its cultivar
-- rows; those rows still match their own printed names. That is the same
-- tradeoff 0021, 0025 and 0028 made.
--
-- Expected: UPDATE 4654. Any other count means the directory moved since the
-- audit — roll back and re-derive the id set.
BEGIN;

